<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<jmeterTestPlan version="1.2" properties="5.0" jmeter="5.6.3">
  <hashTree>
    <TestPlan guiclass="TestPlanGui" testclass="TestPlan" testname="Account Read — Spike (recovery test)" enabled="true">
      <stringProp name="TestPlan.comments">Spike test: surge the read endpoint from 70% boundary up to 200%
boundary, hold 3 min, then drop back to 70% to observe recovery.

╔══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╗
║ BOUNDARY (from realistic-pacing stress run): 650 VUs                 ║
║                                                                      ║
║   BOUNDARY      = 650 VUs   (last fully clean step in the timer-     ║
║                              enabled stress run)                     ║
║   70% BOUNDARY  = 455 VUs   (warm-up / recovery level)               ║
║   SPIKE (200%)  = 1300 VUs  (the surge target)                       ║
║                                                                      ║
║ Sized at 200% (not 150%) on the same rationale as the withdraw /     ║
║ deposit spike plans: 150% would just replay the load test's          ║
║ just-over phase rather than test true surge tolerance.               ║
║                                                                      ║
║ The earlier 3-5 VU "boundary" measured without the Gaussian timer    ║
║ was a load-generator artefact (Windows ephemeral-port exhaustion at  ║
║ high connection churn for fast reads). With the timer applied in the ║
║ new stress run, the true realistic-pacing server capacity is two     ║
║ orders of magnitude higher.                                          ║
║                                                                      ║
║ ⚠ Load-generator memory: 1300 JVM threads × ~1 MB stack each ≈       ║
║ 1.3 GB JMeter heap just for thread stacks at the spike peak. The     ║
║ default JMeter heap (1 GB) WILL OOM during the spike ramp. Set       ║
║ HEAP="-Xms1g -Xmx4g" in the JMeter launcher before running.          ║
║                                                                      ║
║ ⚠ Spike ramp rate: +845 threads over 5s = ~169 threads/s             ║
║ instantiation rate. JMeter handles this but the ramp briefly         ║
║ stresses the load generator's scheduler. If you see ramp jitter or   ║
║ delayed thread starts, extend r2's startup from 5s to 10s and        ║
║ reduce its delay from 310s to 305s to keep phase alignment.          ║
╚══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╝

Virtual users are spread across MANY distinct accounts (tests/stress/data/accounts.csv,
1000 rows) via CSVDataSet round-robin (shareMode.all + recycle), so single-row
read contention is NOT a factor. We're measuring system-wide surge response,
not single-account behaviour.

This exercises the READ path: plain SELECT, no FOR UPDATE, no $transaction,
no transactions-table write.

Shape (Ultimate Thread Group, 13:30 total):

  ┌──────────────┬──────┬───────┬─────────────────────────────────┐
  │ Window       │ VUs  │ Hold  │ Maps to spec                    │
  ├──────────────┼──────┼───────┼─────────────────────────────────┤
  │ 0–5 min      │  455 │  5 m  │ 70% boundary    (warm-up)       │
  │ 5–8 min      │ 1300 │  3 m  │ 200% boundary   (the SPIKE)     │
  │ 8–13:30 min  │  455 │  5 m  │ 70% boundary    (recovery)      │
  └──────────────┴──────┴───────┴─────────────────────────────────┘

Pass criteria:
  - During warm-up and recovery: p95 &lt; 5000ms, error rate &lt; 1%
  - During the spike: errors and elevated latency are acceptable —
    what we measure is whether the system DEGRADES GRACEFULLY (returns
    proper 5xx, drains its queues, keeps the event loop responsive)
    and RECOVERS to baseline once the spike ends. A spike that leaves
    the system degraded after VUs drop back to 14 is a failure even
    if the spike phase itself looked OK.

Response Assertion: assume_success=true — the assertion still requires
exactly 200, so any 403 / 404 / 5xx / timeout counts as an error.
assume_success=true means only assertion failures and client/transport
errors count toward the budget; this is consistent with the load plan.

Think time: a Gaussian Random Timer is attached to the sampler (mean
3000ms, deviation 1000ms). Even with think time, the spike phase
represents a real surge: 2× as many CONCURRENT USERS hitting the
system simultaneously. Real-world spikes (flash sales, news events)
look like a sudden influx of normal-paced users — not the same users
firing 100× more requests. The timer makes this test measure the
realistic version of that scenario, consistent with the load plan
and the (now timer-enabled) stress plan.

Consequence: with the timer, the BindException risk that previously
dominated this test effectively disappears. 8 VUs × ~0.3 req/s ≈
2.4 req/s aggregate — three orders of magnitude under the ~68 conn/s
Windows ephemeral-port ceiling. The previous BindException-driven
"breaking point" reflected load-generator saturation, not the server.
This run should walk cleanly through warm-up → spike → recovery with
essentially no errors — if so, that is the finding (realistic surge
absorbed). Raise the spike VU count to actually stress the surge
response if every phase runs clean.

Run headless from PROJECT ROOT:
  jmeter -n -t tests/stress/jmeter/spike/account-read.spike.jmx \
         -l tests/stress/reports/account-read-spike.jtl \
         -e -o tests/stress/reports/account-read-spike-html

Or via the wrapper script:
  npm run test:spike:account-read

Prereqs:
  1. npx tsx tests/stress/scripts/seed.ts
  2. Server running on localhost:3000 (npm run build &amp;&amp; npm start)
  3. JMeter Plugin: jpgc-casutg (Custom Thread Groups)</stringProp>
      <boolProp name="TestPlan.functional_mode">false</boolProp>
      <boolProp name="TestPlan.serialize_threadgroups">false</boolProp>
      <elementProp name="TestPlan.user_defined_variables" elementType="Arguments" guiclass="ArgumentsPanel" testclass="Arguments" testname="User Defined Variables" enabled="true">
        <collectionProp name="Arguments.arguments">
          <elementProp name="BASE_URL" elementType="Argument">
            <stringProp name="Argument.name">BASE_URL</stringProp>
            <stringProp name="Argument.value">${__P(host,localhost)}</stringProp>
            <stringProp name="Argument.metadata">=</stringProp>
          </elementProp>
          <elementProp name="PORT" elementType="Argument">
            <stringProp name="Argument.name">PORT</stringProp>
            <stringProp name="Argument.value">${__P(port,3000)}</stringProp>
            <stringProp name="Argument.metadata">=</stringProp>
          </elementProp>
          <elementProp name="DATA_DIR" elementType="Argument">
            <stringProp name="Argument.name">DATA_DIR</stringProp>
            <stringProp name="Argument.value">${__P(dataDir,tests/stress/data)}</stringProp>
            <stringProp name="Argument.metadata">=</stringProp>
          </elementProp>
        </collectionProp>
      </elementProp>
      <stringProp name="TestPlan.user_define_classpath"></stringProp>
    </TestPlan>
    <hashTree>
